    {
      "id": "claim_36a21b729b51426293edff9194df2f6f",
      "statement": "Before processing, the handler verifies ownership via prisma.userArmy.findFirst scoped to req.userId and returns 404 NOT_FOUND otherwise; it then upserts user_unit_media on the composite unique constraint @@unique([rosterId, unitInstanceId]), so a unit instance holds at most one custom photo whose imageUrl/thumbnailUrl/storageKeyPrefix are replaced in place on re-upload. The row cascades from UserProfile and UserArmy, so roster deletion removes media rows without touching bucket objects.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/api/src/routes/media.ts#L50-L95",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8f68fa97426b796658d11326313ff0f6e945633ba28c187c2ee8c187d0ac9b8a:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://packages/db-client/prisma/schema.prisma#L277-L295",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6e80eb74722ca1e59344ee37343b23e011d01ff3f0cdb2e7b5dacd30e4526150:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_077c2382ad384ddd8859b38203d41122",
      "statement": "Media deletion resolves the row by media id or by (rosterId, unitInstanceId) always scoped to userId, deletes card.webp and thumb.webp from object storage in a Promise.all, and only then deletes the Postgres row, with no transaction or compensation across the two systems. A storage failure aborts before the DB delete (client sees 500 DELETE_ERROR with possibly one object already removed), while a DB failure after storage success orphans a row whose URLs no longer resolve; symmetrically, a failed upload upsert or partial PutObject leaves objects with no row, and nothing in the path garbage-collects them.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/api/src/routes/media.ts#L121-L161",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cd83045517bdcd2e5c70745e820635fdb0ded477a16c61a37ce97226a64c4219:"
        },
